---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
import { allPieces } from "../lib/catalog";
import { allLineups, sungOn } from "../lib/vespers";

const pieces = allPieces().length;
const lineups = allLineups();
const firstYear = lineups[0] ? sungOn(lineups[0]).slice(0, 4) : "";
const lastYear = lineups.at(-1) ? sungOn(lineups.at(-1)!).slice(0, 4) : "";
---

<Layout title="About" description="What Cantus Organi is for, how to use it at the organ, and where its material comes from.">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="crumb small muted ui"><a href="/">Cantus Organi</a></p>
    <h1>About Cantus Organi</h1>
    <p class="lede">The organ accompaniment of the Graduale, for the Traditional Latin Mass and Vespers, at the console.</p>

    <h2 id="what">What it is for</h2>
    <p>
      <em>Nova Organi Harmonia ad Graduale Romanum</em> was published by the Lemmens Institute at
      Mechelen in 1942: eight volumes of organ accompaniment for the whole of the Gregorian
      repertoire of the Mass and of Sunday Vespers. It is in the public domain, but in print it is
      rare and scattered, and finding the right page for a given Sunday means knowing which volume
      holds it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site puts the scanned pages online, divided into {pieces} pieces, and ties them to the
      calendar of the 1962 Missal, so an organist can go from a date to the music for that day's
      Mass and Vespers. Nothing is re-engraved: every system of music is an image of the printed
      page, with its printed page number.
    </p>

    <h2 id="how">How to use it</h2>
    <h3>Find the music for a day</h3>
    <p>
      Open the <a href="/calendar/">Calendar</a> and choose a date. The day's page lists the Proper
      of the Mass (Introit, Gradual, Alleluia or Tract, Offertory, Communion), the Ordinary it
      suggests, and its Vespers. Each part has a link straight to its place in the music.
    </p>
    <h3>Vespers</h3>
    <p>
      For Sundays and feasts, the <a href="/vespers/">Vespers</a> pages give the whole office in
      the order sung — the opening versicle, the five psalms with their antiphons, the chapter,
      hymn, versicle, Magnificat and the seasonal Marian antiphon — built for each date from
      {firstYear} to {lastYear}. I Vespers of the greater feasts have their own page, for the
      evening before. Where the book prints only the opening of a psalm, the page says which
      formula to continue with and gives the psalm's text; where it prints no accompaniment in a
      tone at all, the page says so rather than guess.
    </p>
    <h3>Browse the books</h3>
    <p>
      The <a href="/">home page</a> lists the books as printed: the Proper of Time, the Proper of
      Saints, the Common, the <a href="/kyriale/">Kyriale</a>, the Masses for the Dead, the
      Vesperale, and votive Masses. <a href="/search/">Search</a> finds a piece by its title or
      opening words.
    </p>
    <h3>At the organ</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Invert</strong> (top of every page) turns the scans white on black, for a dark
        organ loft. The setting is remembered on that device.</li>
      <li><strong>Export PDF</strong> makes a file of the pages you need, to print or to keep on a
        tablet on the music desk.</li>
      <li><strong>Chant</strong> links open the melody on GregoBase; on pages that offer it, the
        switch <em>Show the chant</em> draws the chant above the accompaniment.</li>
      <li>Pages print cleanly: the menus and buttons are left off the paper.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="limits">What it does not do yet</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Only the 1962 rubrics. Earlier and later calendars are not followed.</li>
      <li>Vespers is built for Sundays and I and II class feasts that the book prints. A few feasts
        that fall on a Sunday only in some years (the Transfiguration, the Holy Cross, St Michael)
        have no Vespers page, because the book has no section for them.</li>
      <li>Some Magnificat and psalm tones are not printed in the Vesperale; those are marked on the
        page to be sung unaccompanied or improvised.</li>
      <li>Parts of the catalogue were matched by machine and have not all been checked by eye.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="corrections">Errors and corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If a piece is on the wrong page, a title or mode is wrong, or a chant link points at the
      wrong melody, please say so on the <a href="/corrections/">Corrections</a> page. Every report
      is read and fixed by hand.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sources">Sources and attributions</h2>
    <dl class="sources">
      <dt><em>Nova Organi Harmonia ad Graduale Romanum</em></dt>
      <dd>Lemmens Institute, Mechelen: H. Dessain, 1942. The page images and everything cut from
        them are in the <strong>public domain</strong>.</dd>

      <dt><a href="https://gregobase.selapa.net">GregoBase</a></dt>
      <dd>The chant melodies shown with each part and linked from it.
        Released under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/">CC0</a>.</dd>

      <dt><a href="https://github.com/bbloomf/exsurge">Exsurge</a></dt>
      <dd>Draws the chant notation in the browser. MIT licence, © 2016 Fr. Matthew Spencer, OSJ;
        maintained by Benjamin Bloomfield.</dd>

      <dt><a href="https://github.com/bbloomf/jgabc">jgabc</a></dt>
      <dd>Which chants make up each day's Proper. The Unlicense (public domain).</dd>

      <dt><a href="https://github.com/mmolenda/missalemeum">Missalemeum</a></dt>
      <dd>The 1962 calendar: which Mass is said on each date. MIT licence.</dd>

      <dt><a href="https://github.com/DivinumOfficium/divinum-officium">Divinum Officium</a></dt>
      <dd>The texts of Vespers — antiphons, psalms, chapters and hymns — and which are said on each
        day. MIT licence.</dd>

      <dt><a href="https://github.com/jsrjenkins/vesperale">vesperale</a></dt>
      <dd>Fr. John Jenkins's Vespers booklets, whose table of Magnificat antiphons and tones was
        used to check this site's.</dd>

      <dt><a href="https://pagefind.app">Pagefind</a></dt>
      <dd>The site search. MIT licence.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      The site itself, its code and its hand-made indexes of the books are released under
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, and are on
      <a href="https://github.com/npadley/cantusorgani">GitHub</a>, so the work can be copied and
      carried on.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .crumb { margin: 0 0 var(--s-1); }
  .crumb a { color: var(--ink-muted); }
  .lede { font-style: italic; font-size: var(--step-1); }
  h2 { margin-top: var(--s-5); }
  .sources dt { margin-top: var(--s-3); font-weight: 600; }
  .sources dd { margin: var(--s-1) 0 0; }
</style>
